### Enhancing Resilience with Resilience4j

The "Implement Observability & Monitoring" and "Implement Inference & Orchestration Layer" stages underscore the need for robust, fault-tolerant systems. AI models can be resource-intensive, prone to transient errors, or hosted on external services that might experience outages. Resilience4j provides a lightweight, functional approach to introduce resilience patterns like Circuit Breaker, Retry, Rate Limiter, and Bulkhead into your AI-native APIs.

Implementing a Circuit Breaker pattern with Resilience4j can protect your application from repeatedly trying to invoke a failing AI model service, preventing cascading failures and providing graceful degradation.

Here's an example of applying a Resilience4j Circuit Breaker to an AI model invocation:

```java
import io.resilience4j.circuitbreaker.CircuitBreaker;
import io.resilience4j.circuitbreaker.CircuitBreakerConfig;
import io.resilience4j.circuitbreaker.CircuitBreakerRegistry;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import java.time.Duration;
import java.util.function.Supplier;

@Service
public class ResilientAiModelService {

    private final CircuitBreaker circuitBreaker;
    private final AiModelService aiModelService; // The actual AI model service

    public ResilientAiModelService(AiModelService aiModelService) {
        this.aiModelService = aiModelService;

        // Configure a Circuit Breaker
        CircuitBreakerConfig config = CircuitBreakerConfig.custom()
            .failureRateThreshold(50) // Percentage of failures before opening the circuit
            .waitDurationInOpenState(Duration.ofSeconds(60)) // Time in open state
            .ringBufferSizeInHalfOpenState(10) // Number of calls in half-open state
            .ringBufferSizeInClosedState(100) // Number of calls in closed state
            .build();

        CircuitBreakerRegistry registry = CircuitBreakerRegistry.of(config);
        this.circuitBreaker = registry.circuitBreaker("aiModelInferenceCircuit");

        // Register event listeners for observability
        circuitBreaker.getEventPublisher()
            .onStateTransition(event -> System.out.println("Circuit Breaker State Transition: " + event.getOldState() + " -> " + event.getNewState()))
            .onError(event -> System.err.println("Circuit Breaker Error: " + event.getThrowable().getMessage()))
            .onCallNotPermitted(event -> System.warn("Circuit Breaker: Call not permitted. Circuit is open."));
    }

    public InferenceResult performResilientInference(InferenceRequest request) {
        Supplier<InferenceResult> inferenceSupplier = () -> aiModelService.performInference(request);

        // Decorate the supplier with the Circuit Breaker
        Supplier<InferenceResult> decoratedSupplier = CircuitBreaker.decorateSupplier(circuitBreaker, inferenceSupplier);

        try {
            return decoratedSupplier.get();
        } catch (io.resilience4j.circuitbreaker.CallNotPermittedException e) {
            System.err.println("Circuit breaker is open, falling back for request: " + request.getRequestId());
            // Implement a fallback mechanism, e.g., return a default/cached result, or a simpler model's output
            return new InferenceResult(request.getRequestId(), "Fallback: Service unavailable.");
        } catch (Exception e) {
            System.err.println("Error during resilient inference for request " + request.getRequestId() + ": " + e.getMessage());
            throw new RuntimeException("Inference failed", e); // Re-throw or handle as appropriate
        }
    }
}
